WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.560
I like making decks that make people question&nbsp;
their preconceptions about deckbuilding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.200
and about the EDH format as a whole. And one&nbsp;
of my favorite examples of this is my Glissa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:13.400
deck. It is a battlecruiser deck that uses&nbsp;
its command zone to give it a consistent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.760
high-quality early creature, thereby allowing the&nbsp;
cards in the 99 to be a pile of greedy, high-cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.760
nonsense. Just look at that beautiful monster&nbsp;
of a curve. This sort of massive-nonsense-tribal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:29.040
deck is really fun for when I don’t wanna&nbsp;
play a deck that’s particularly challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.880
and would prefer to just indulge my battlecruiser&nbsp;
desires. But unfortunately, there’s a problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.520
This deck plays a shitload of removal to disrupt&nbsp;
opposing gameplans, and this works out quite well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.920
for the deck’s general gameplan—ramping to a&nbsp;
double digit land count as fast as possible and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:50.400
then turning on the boss music. But this gameplan&nbsp;
can be a bit… contentious at times. Not so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.720
that I never play it, but enough so that it can’t&nbsp;
be my default mid-power fun deck for when I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.400
wanna think too hard—people get annoyed if I spend&nbsp;
too much time as the final boss. All this to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.000
for the past 6 months I’ve been vaguely&nbsp;
looking around for a battlecruiser deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.800
that might be able to scratch similar itches&nbsp;
while being a bit less brutal to play against.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.880
A little while back, somebody mentioned to&nbsp;
me the idea of running Radha, Heir to Keld as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.360
a commander, and this got my noggin joggin’. You&nbsp;
know what sucks to draw in the late game? Low cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:23.680
ramp spells. Any ramp deck worth its salt needs&nbsp;
to coordinate the ratio of ramp to other stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.600
and also the ratio of low cost ramp to high&nbsp;
cost ramp. Running a lot of low-cost ramp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.200
leads to beautiful curve-outs and buttery-smooth&nbsp;
early-midgames, but it also leads to running out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.680
of cards faster and worsens the quality of later&nbsp;
game topdecks. I built a Goreclaw deck awhile back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:44.160
that ran 16 low cost ramp spells, with the goal&nbsp;
to land Goreclaw turn 2 or 3 and then vomit out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.600
a massive amount of power and toughness on turns&nbsp;
4-5. The deck did a great job of accomplishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:55.080
exactly that task, but it pretty much either won&nbsp;
quickly or ran out of steam after eating a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.080
removal spells or a board wipe. Running a lot of&nbsp;
draw is the best way to avoid this in a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:05.320
runs a lot of low-cost dorks, but despite my best&nbsp;
efforts I couldn’t manage to cram the necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.800
amount of draw into the deck without incurring&nbsp;
a substantial cost to the beefiness of the deck.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.520
On the opposite end of the ramp spectrum,&nbsp;
there’s the option of running a lot of high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.600
cost ramp instead. This preserves topdeck quality&nbsp;
and lessens the number of cards needed to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.960
the same degree of ramp. However it will lead&nbsp;
to slower starts and fewer keepable hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.440
worsening a deck’s mulligan phase and its ability&nbsp;
to get off the ground. But what if there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.760
way to do the inverse of what was done in the&nbsp;
Goreclaw deck—to have the commander cover the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.560
low-cost ramp, and then run a massive pile of&nbsp;
4-cost ramp? This is where Radha comes back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:44.600
in as a two-drop mana dork that’s available at&nbsp;
all times. One potential Radha build could be to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:51.120
run a lot of 4-5 cost plays, but these tend to&nbsp;
be a lot worse than 6-7 cost cards. Dropping a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.520
generically high quality four drop on turn 3 such&nbsp;
as Forgotten Ancient or Atsushi is a lot less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.680
impressive than dropping a Kogla or an Etali,&nbsp;
Primal Storm on turn 4, and creatures that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.920
six or more mana also tend to stay relevant&nbsp;
as topdecks, whereas 4-5 mana creatures are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.440
by contrast often kinda disappointing to topdeck&nbsp;
later in the game. This is not to say that we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:17.800
gonna be trying to let the deck get to the point&nbsp;
where what’s on top of its library is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.360
but when you’re playing a cheap red-green&nbsp;
midrange deck, you gotta know that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.320
will be games where you don’t find enough of your&nbsp;
draw and end up relying on the heart of the cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.920
And in those cases, you’ll usually be glad to&nbsp;
draw a Dragonlair Spider instead of an Arasta.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.120
So this was my start in building Radha—4 cost&nbsp;
ramp that I can play on turn 3. There are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.280
different options here, including some really&nbsp;
potent mana dorks that might allow for an extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.720
explosive turn 4-5, but I made my decision&nbsp;
of what to put here largely with the intent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.880
of hedging around a potential weakness for the&nbsp;
deck. See, green stompy decks tend to struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:57.200
with one type of card above all others—board&nbsp;
wipes. And while mana dorks often allow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.480
faster acceleration than other types of ramp,&nbsp;
they have the downside that they generally get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.960
eaten by board wipes alongside the big threats&nbsp;
that prompted the wipe. A big goal for this deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.720
was being able ramp relatively quickly into&nbsp;
6 and 7 drops, but also being able to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:16.800
through hate and keep scaling into the late&nbsp;
game. The ramp suite I ended up landing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.800
focused on a classic—Explosive Vegetation.&nbsp;
I run all 10 of these effects that exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.480
as well as Blighted Woodland, an untapped land&nbsp;
that can also serve as turn 3 ramp if I activate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.360
it during combat after attacking with Radha. From&nbsp;
there the deck ventures into a bunch of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:38.080
cards that perform some sort of ramp function,&nbsp;
including extra land spells like Mina and Denn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.640
filtering cards that produce treasures such as&nbsp;
Unexpected Windfall, and a handful of other cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:48.000
Since I want some sort of ramp on turn 3 every&nbsp;
single game to allow me to play a six drop on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.800
turn 4, I opted for 19 total cards that offer turn&nbsp;
3 ramp. That gives a 79% chance of drawing one in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.840
each opening hand, a figure that is high enough to&nbsp;
leave some room for needing to mulligan for lands.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.760
With my core idea for the deck consisting of&nbsp;
Radha and a pile of 4 cost ramp, I had a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.960
on my hands: how much low-cost stuff should I&nbsp;
run? I knew that it wouldn’t be a big amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:20.080
and I eventually decided on literally none at&nbsp;
all, and this is for one key reason: cascade. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.240
big downside of cascade is how luck-based it&nbsp;
is. When you play a five cost cascade card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.400
it might hit a four cost card, but it also&nbsp;
might hit a one-cost card. However, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.800
simply don’t run any cards that cost less than 4,&nbsp;
you’re guaranteed a pretty decent minimum level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.920
of value from your cascade trigger. This fact&nbsp;
paired with my density of ramp on four means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.040
cascades and discovers are actually reasonably&nbsp;
predictable. Cascading for a four-or-less-cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:54.240
card yields an 85% chance of finding a card with&nbsp;
some sort of ramp attached, and cascading for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.400
a five-or-less-cost card yields a two thirds&nbsp;
chance of finding specifically a ramp spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:05.400
but an additional 14% chance of finding a card&nbsp;
that cascades for four, which I must remind you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:11.320
has a 85% chance of finding a ramp spell.&nbsp;
Cascading for a six-or-less-cost card has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:16.600
a more modest 40% chance of finding a ramp&nbsp;
spell, but if we account for the cascade and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:22.960
discover cards this ends up getting boosted up to&nbsp;
a 55% chance. What this silly jumble of numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:28.520
means in practice is that the deck is effectively&nbsp;
running a high-20s number of ramp spells, and all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:35.440
of them ramp for at least two mana or have other&nbsp;
upside. Translation: we’re in lands town, baby.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.880
So what does the deck spend the resulting mana&nbsp;
on? As I alluded to earlier, a big place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.840
spend this mana is on 6-7 mana creatures, as&nbsp;
these work well both late and as a juicy turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:52.960
4 play. A lot of these are exactly what you’d&nbsp;
expect—Etali Primal Storm, Rampaging Baloths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:58.360
a decent handful of cascade creatures—but some&nbsp;
others are a bit further off the beaten track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.440
and this is motivated by a couple factors. For&nbsp;
one, I wanted to build this deck on a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.880
tight budget, both for the sake of my wallet and&nbsp;
to force me into some more creative directions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:15.360
so nothing over $4. Secondly, the deck&nbsp;
ramps very hard and has a very high curve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.080
and there are cards that work well with this.&nbsp;
One such card is Screamer Killer, a card from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:26.640
the Warhammer 40k set that’s shockingly close to&nbsp;
being a Terror of the Peaks in my deck, but at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.360
tiny fraction of the price of that fancy dragon.&nbsp;
Another favorite of mine is Emergent Woodwurm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:37.280
a card that’s so underplayed and causes such a&nbsp;
large swing in board the turn it enters that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.360
frequently have opponents do a double take and ask&nbsp;
“wait, that card does what?” I also run Tyrant’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:48.360
Familiar and Thunderfoot Baloth. These are quite&nbsp;
powerful with a commander in play, but most decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.000
can’t take full advantage of them because&nbsp;
opponents can simply remove their commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.800
before attacks, which ends up being a soft two for&nbsp;
one. However, since my commander is a mana dork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.200
that I don’t really care about past turn 4 or 5,&nbsp;
my opponents are far less eager to remove her, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.280
she’s pretty cheap to replay to get the lieutenant&nbsp;
ability back online if they do remove her.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.400
A big theme among the rest of the Radha deck’s&nbsp;
high-cost creatures is something that it shares&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.520
with my Glissa deck—a decent portion of the&nbsp;
deck’s creatures have some sort of removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:21.560
attached. This is great, as it allows the deck to&nbsp;
pack a lot of ways to deal with opposing threats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.840
while also allowing the deck to put on&nbsp;
pressure by having a large density of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:30.280
big creatures that it starts playing on turn&nbsp;
4. The deck also packs some amount of draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.680
but far less than you’d expect. There are 7&nbsp;
engines and 2 one-time draw spells, and while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:40.600
playing the first dozen or so games with the deck,&nbsp;
I’ve kept expecting to need to add more draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:45.200
but it just hasn’t been necessary—the deck does&nbsp;
a perfectly fine job maintaining resources in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:50.320
its hand. This once again goes back to the&nbsp;
mana curve—even though the deck gets massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:54.800
land counts, since roughly a third of the&nbsp;
deck is creatures that cost 5 or more mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:00.240
it often stocks up a hand full of large threats&nbsp;
earlier in the game, giving it plenty of time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.760
find a draw engine as it builds up its land count.&nbsp;
This is supplemented by the cascade-Explosive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.880
Vegetation plan, which thins the deck of lands&nbsp;
and to a lesser extent ramp spells, helping it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.120
to draw into more big threats later. I’m broadly&nbsp;
not too impressed by the effect of deck thinning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.160
as a couple fewer lands won’t have a wildly&nbsp;
meaningful impact on a deck that is looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:26.520
to win by turn 8 or 9. Scale this up though,&nbsp;
and pulling 8 or more lands out of a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:32.400
that is looking to grind way into the late game&nbsp;
does start to become decidedly more impactful.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.480
The confluence of the Radha deck’s intense&nbsp;
density of ramp, vast number of high cost threats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:43.040
and adequate quantity of draw ends up being a&nbsp;
deck that stockpiles all game resources at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.760
same time—it develops its board while ramping&nbsp;
hard and maintaining plenty of resources in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:54.760
hand. And this fact leads to the deck’s biggest&nbsp;
strength—its flexibility. Against faster decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:59.440
it can dig in its heels and focus on staying&nbsp;
alive until it has the resources to stabilize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:04.840
knowing it’ll easily outscale them later in the&nbsp;
game, whereas against decks that go bigger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:09.520
it in the late game it can advance a bunch of&nbsp;
large creatures and turn them sideways to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.440
that deck down to a manageable life total&nbsp;
before it gets scary. The deck also plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:19.000
through most hate just fine—it doesn’t&nbsp;
run significant numbers of artifacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:24.600
enchantments, graveyard synergies, or pivotal&nbsp;
singular creatures. Board wipes are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:30.200
the only commonly-played hate in EDH that causes&nbsp;
problems for a turbo-ramping big creature deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:35.320
and the deck plays decently well through these,&nbsp;
too. Advancing a few threats is all that’s needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:39.680
to have a boardwipe-worthy board state, and&nbsp;
this generally leaves plenty of beefy stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:45.320
in hand and far more lands than opponents&nbsp;
for purposes of playing them out. The deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.440
cascade creatures are also a thing that feels&nbsp;
perfectly fine to play into a board wipe, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:54.440
they usually find some sort of ramp that can help&nbsp;
the deck bounce back more quickly after the wipe.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.920
Before I spend too much time indulgently hyping up&nbsp;
my deck, I should talk about the downsides of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:04.960
deck. For one, it lacks basically any instant&nbsp;
speed interaction—if I’m being generous it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:10.720
a grand total of four instant speed removal&nbsp;
cards and one instant speed protection card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:14.640
This is mostly a product of the deck being&nbsp;
a cascade deck that runs no cards below 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:20.880
mana—the desirable options for instant-speed&nbsp;
interaction are pretty slim. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:26.480
the deck is extremely consistent in early turns,&nbsp;
an attribute that comes with both positives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.320
negatives. The positive is that as long as it&nbsp;
mulligans properly, the deck basically never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:38.120
has do-nothing games. The downside is twofold—for&nbsp;
one, it can be a little boring, since the deck has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:43.680
basically no meaningful decisions between the&nbsp;
mulligan phase and turn 4. The other issue is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:48.040
that it’s predictable, since opponents who have&nbsp;
played against the deck know its rough curve out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:53.520
of the first four turns. The fact that the deck&nbsp;
is locked into this curve-out is another reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:58.760
why I opted for the $50 budget—it literally&nbsp;
cannot increase the speed of its curve-outs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:04.200
so any deck that’s doing something substantially&nbsp;
more relevant on turn 4 than playing a 6 or 7 drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:10.400
beater is too far above the deck’s power level to&nbsp;
be worth trying to match. The deck also requires a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:14.680
very aggressive mulligan policy to make sure&nbsp;
that it is able to get off the ground okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:20.280
though at least it usually does fine starting&nbsp;
with 5 or 6 cards. All of these downsides are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.840
pretty fundamentally tied to the deck’s core&nbsp;
construction—having a deck with no cards costing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:30.400
less than 4 mana still curve out at a decent speed&nbsp;
requires making some concessions here and there.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:35.360
At the end of the day, the process of building&nbsp;
this deck was the process of chasing a vibe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.800
It’s the vibe that I was chasing with my first&nbsp;
commander deck, it’s the vibe I was chasing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:43.880
when I made Glissacruiser, and it’s a vibe that&nbsp;
I’ll hopefully call it quits with chasing now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:50.440
I have this Radha deck. This vibe of having all of&nbsp;
the resources, of being the big bad at the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.240
of playing midrange to the bleeding edge of&nbsp;
what that term can possibly mean—I just find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:59.760
it super fun, and I think it makes for a cool&nbsp;
table dynamic as long as the deck in question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.240
has enough interaction. To me, building a deck&nbsp;
that satisfies a particular feeling is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:10.040
the best parts of casual commander, especially&nbsp;
if the resulting deck ends up being strange and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:15.640
unique. The challenge of making a deck that is&nbsp;
conceptually cool, enjoyable to play, and good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:22.000
for table dynamics is a fun one to navigate, and&nbsp;
when it works out the results can be really great.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.440
That’s all for this video, thanks for&nbsp;
watching! I’ll be posting an endnote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:29.320
to my Patreon tomorrow where I discuss some&nbsp;
of the weirder card choices in the deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:34.280
didn’t make it into the video. That’s right,&nbsp;
I didn’t even talk about the caves package.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:38.400
I’m also currently taking submissions for a&nbsp;
bonus video later this month where I give my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:42.720
thoughts on three patron decks, so if you want&nbsp;
a chance of having your deck reviewed by me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:46.920
you can sign up for my Patreon and&nbsp;
submit your deck within the next week.

